                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 0
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                        %mo )
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    WF(\Delta_H) = \left\{ (x,k) \,\vert\, {\vert x\vert}^2_\eta = 0 \;\text{and} \; {\vert k\vert}^2_\eta = 0 \; \text{and} \;
                    \\left(
                    \\pm k_0 \gt 0 \;\Leftrightarrow\; \pm x^0 \gt 0
                    \\right)
                    \\right\}
            %center
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanPropagator.png", :width => "60"}/
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "Hadamard+distribution#Radzikowski96"}> Radzikowski 96, (16)
            )
          .proof
            %h6#proof_13 Proof
            %p First regarding the causal propagator:
            %p
              By prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SingularSupportOfCausalPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetimeIsTheLightCone"}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support"} singular support
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_S
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light+cone"} light cone
            %p
              Since the causal propagator is a solution to the homogeneous Klein-Gordon equation, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of singularities theorem
              says that also all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vectors"} wave vectors
              in the wave front set are lightlike. Hence it just remains to show that all non-vanishing lightlike wave vectors based on the lightcone in spacetime indeed do appear in the wave front set.
            %p
              To that end, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi cp
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in C^\infty_{cp}(\mathbb{R}^{p,1})
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
              includes the origin.
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in \mathbb{R}^{p,1}
              a point on the light cone, we need to determine the decay property of the Fourier transform of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto b(x-a)\Delta_S(x)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+of+distributions"} convolution of distributions
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi μ
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat b(k)e^{i k_\mu a^\mu}
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ^
                        %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat \Delta_S(k)
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalPropagatorAsFourierTransformOfDeltaDistributionOnTransformedKGOperator"}
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo ^
                      %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∝
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %mo −
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                            %mfrac
                              %mrow
                                %mi m
                                %mi c
                              %mi ℏ
                          %mo )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mi sgn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\widehat \Delta_{S}(k)
                    \\;\propto\;
                    \\delta\left( -k_\mu k^\mu - \left( \tfrac{m c}{\hbar} \right)^2 \right)
                    sgn(k_0)
                    \\,.
            %p
              This means that the convolution product is the smearing of the mass shell by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi b
                        %mo ^
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} u
                          %msup
                            %mi a
                            %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat b(k)e^{i k_\u a^\mu}
            %p
              Since the mass shell asymptotes to the light cone, and since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi μ
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{i k_\mu a^\mu} = 1
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              on the light cone (given that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              is on the light cone), this implies the claim.
            %p
              Now for the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            %p
              By def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StandardHadamardDistributionOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
              its Fourier transform is of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo ^
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ∝
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi m
                        %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\widehat \Delta_H(k)
                    \\;\propto\;
                    \\delta\left( k_\mu k^\mu + m^2 \right) \Theta( -k_0 )
            %p
              Moreover, its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support"} singular support
              is also the light cone (prop.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SingularSupportOfHadamardPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetimeIsTheLightCone"}
            %p
              Therefore now same argument as before says that the wave front set consists of wave vectors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              on the light cone, but now due to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/step+function"} step function
              factor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(-k_0)
              it must satisfy
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq - k_0 = k^0
            %p
              Finally regarding the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            %p
              By prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModeExpansionForFeynmanPropagatorOfKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
              the Feynman propagator coincides with the positive frequency Wightman propagator for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^0 \gt 0
              and with the “negative frequency Hadamard operator” for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^0 \lt 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WF(\Delta_F)
              now follows directly with that of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WF(\Delta_H)
              above.
          %h3#ExampleForDiracOperatorOnMinkowskiSpacetime For Dirac operator on Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            Finally we observe that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            follow immediately from the propagators for the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
          #DiracEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetimeAdvancedAndRetardedPropagators.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_15 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/advanced+and+retarded+propagator"} advanced and retarded propagator
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+equation"} Dirac equation
                on
                = succeed ")" do
